Third-party components, by the app that ships them:

Desktop (Windows, macOS, Linux, FreeBSD)
  Electron                MIT           https://www.electronjs.org
    bundles Chromium and FFmpeg. Their licences ship with every build, in
    LICENSE.electron.txt and LICENSES.chromium.html. On FreeBSD it isn't part
    of this package: it's the system's own, installed from FreeBSD's ports
    tree, with its own licence files in /usr/local/share/licenses. The DejaVu
    fonts come the same way, as Electron brings none there.
  electron-builder        MIT           build tool only, not shipped

Android
  AndroidX: Activity, Core, Lifecycle       Apache-2.0
  Jetpack Compose                           Apache-2.0
  AndroidX Media3 (ExoPlayer, HLS, Session, UI)  Apache-2.0
  ZXing core                                Apache-2.0
    Draws the QR code on the TV sign-in screen.
  Material Symbols (rounded)                Apache-2.0
    The few icons the sign-in screens use, copied in as vector paths.
  Coil                                      Apache-2.0
  OkHttp, Okio                              Apache-2.0
  Kotlin, kotlinx.coroutines,
    kotlinx.serialization                   Apache-2.0
  Bouncy Castle (bcprov)                    MIT         https://www.bouncycastle.org
    For Ed25519, which Android only has itself from API 33. Only that code
    is used, and shrinking leaves out the rest. Its licence is below.
  JUnit 4                EPL-1.0          tests only, not shipped

Apple (Apple TV, iPhone, iPad, Apple Vision Pro, Mac)
  WebRTC (libwebrtc)       BSD-3-Clause     https://webrtc.googlesource.com/src
    as LiveKit's build, LiveKitWebRTC 144.7559.15 (MIT),
    https://github.com/livekit/webrtc-xcframework, for games over WebRTC.
    It adds about 11 MB to the installed app.
    It includes the parts WebRTC builds in (BoringSSL, Opus, libvpx, libyuv,
    libsrtp, usrsctp, Abseil), each under its own permissive licence, listed
    with WebRTC's source.
  Otherwise only Apple's own system frameworks ship in the app.
  XcodeGen                 MIT              build tool only, not shipped

Checked so far: the desktop dependency tree was read from its installed package
files, and everything in it is under a permissive licence. Nothing from it
except Electron ships in the app. The Android and Apple entries are from each
project's published licence and have not been checked against a resolved build,
which should be done the first time those apps compile. Bouncy Castle was checked
in the jar itself: it has no dependencies of its own.

